      [
        "Confidence meets an exception",
        "The fighting reveals contrasting responses to danger. Young Siward dies facing Macbeth. Macbeth initially avoids Macduff, then relies on the assurance about birth. Macduff explains that he was taken prematurely from his mother’s womb. The apparent guarantee collapses: the play’s wording has misled Macbeth’s expectations rather than promised what he assumed."
      ],
      [
        "Resistance ends in a promised restoration",
        "Macbeth refuses the humiliation of capture and fights; Macduff kills him and presents his head. Malcolm invites the assembled supporters to see him crowned at Scone, names the thanes earls, and promises to recall exiles and address the tyranny. The ending proposes restoration; it does not show a long future of flawless government. Folger places the final material in 5.8, while some editions divide 5.8–5.9."
      ]
